2.52.0

Add the data path for L3 unicast route offloading for IPv4 and IPv6 FIB
and nexthop groups.

* FIB entry lifecycle management. FIB entries are mapped to hardware
  using LPM VCAP rules.

* Nexthop group and nexthop lifecycle management. These are mapped to
  hardware using an ARP table for ECMP, and inline vcap actions for
  single nexthops. Each FIB entry has exactly one nexthop group. As a
  simplification each FIB has a copy of its nexthop group, and we do
  not support nexthop objects.

* Neighbour (ARP/NDP) lifecycle management. A neighbour is anchored to
  the router leg it belongs to and referenced by the nexthops that use
  it as a gateway (and shared amongst them). When a neighbour referenced
  by a nexthop is removed we set the HW mac to zero which makes sure
  traffic for the nexthop is trapped. A neighbour also has an associated
  /32 or /128 route in the LPM VCAP for traffic destined directly to the
  neighbour.

Neighbour entries are created as a side effect of nexthop resolution
but are not yet updated from ARP/NDP events. Neighbour event handling
to activate MAC resolution is added in the next patch.

The three lifecycles are handled in a single patch because their CRUD is
mutually entangled. Router leg teardown flushes the legs neighbours,
tearing down each neighbours host route and refreshing the dependent
nexthops, so the leg path calls into the neighbour and nexthop CRUD. The
NETEVENT_NEIGH_UPDATE handler added in the next patch drives the same
neighbour and nexthop helpers. The layers therefore have to land
together for the patch to build and for the teardown and update paths to
stay consistent.

Reviewed-by: Daniel Machon <daniel.machon@microchip.com>
Reviewed-by: Steen Hegelund <Steen.Hegelund@microchip.com>
Signed-off-by: Jens Emil Schulz Østergaard <jensemil.schulzostergaard@microchip.com>
---
Changes in v2:
- Complete the leg-anchoring rework: remove the software
  LPM (fib_lpm_get/lookup/insert/remove, fib_entry_lpm_match,
  neigh_entry_fib_link). FIB-entry teardown no longer touches
  neighbours. Leg teardown flushes the legs neighbours. Reorder
  router_deinit (fib_flush -> legs_flush -> WARN_ON(neigh_ht) -> destroy
  hashtables). Fixes the neighbour-entry leak.
- fib6 nexthop prune: size rt_arr by the old nexthop count and set the
  count from what was actually copied, fixing a heap overflow.
- router_init() error path: flush FIB entries before destroying the FIB
  hashtable, fixing a leak of entries queued by the initial dump.
